8
00:00:15.428 --> 00:00:22.340
Ch 05, Ch 13, Ch 20, Ch 23, Ch 36, What you will build:

9
00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.625
The grocery platform's payment system:

10
00:00:24.905 --> 00:00:35.700
UPI and card checkouts through a Razorpay-style gateway, the customer wallet, refunds, reconciliation with the gateway's settlement files, and routing across gateways.

11
00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:49.484
Earlier chapters promised the pieces, idempotency keys (Ch 20), sagas with a NEEDSATTENTION state (Ch 13), ledger invariants (Ch 05), tokenisation (Ch 23).

12
00:00:49.764 --> 00:00:54.201
This chapter assembles them into a system where every rupee is accounted for.

13
00:00:54.481 --> 00:00:58.044
L1: money under concurrency (real PostgreSQL).

14
00:00:58.324 --> 00:01:01.887
20,000 wallet operations ran on 16 threads.

15
00:01:03.237 --> 00:01:13.071
A read-then-write balance column left 116 of 200 wallets wrong and silently created 34,011.55 rupees.

16
00:01:13.351 --> 00:01:19.261
Row locks plus a double-entry journal were exact, at ~4,100 ops per second.

17
00:01:19.541 --> 00:01:25.535
Deriving balances from the journal under SERIALIZABLE was also exact, but hit a retry storm:

18
00:01:25.815 --> 00:01:31.170
58,764 retries, 712 ops per second.

19
00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:34.031
L2: a real payment flow with webhooks.

20
00:01:34.311 --> 00:01:44.274
A fake Razorpay-style gateway ran over HTTP, with webhooks that were duplicated, reordered and 5% dropped, plus some tampered app callbacks.

21
00:01:45.624 --> 00:01:56.355
Trusting the app and applying every webhook fulfilled 1,621 orders twice and 35 unpaid, and left 106 paid payments marked FAILED.

22
00:01:56.635 --> 00:02:05.317
A guarded state machine with event dedupe removed all of that, but left 87 paid customers unfulfilled because their webhook never came.

23
00:02:05.597 --> 00:02:12.616
A reconciliation poller brought the result to 1,708 of 1,708 correct.

24
00:02:12.896 --> 00:02:15.435
L3: daily settlement reconciliation.

25
00:02:15.715 --> 00:02:23.139
Across 103,000 transactions with 232 real problems and 500 benign differences:

26
00:02:23.419 --> 00:02:25.232
Totals alone explained nothing.

27
00:02:26.582 --> 00:02:34.774
Exact line matching raised 582 breaks, 500 of them false alarms, and found only 82 real problems.

28
00:02:35.054 --> 00:02:50.414
Rules for settlement delays, rounding, fees and duplicates produced a queue of exactly the 232 real exceptions worth 93,130. rupeesThe largest is 40 customers charged with no order.

29
00:02:50.694 --> 00:02:52.465
L4: whose failure is it?

30
00:02:52.745 --> 00:02:57.545
In 2 hours of UPI traffic there was a gateway incident and a bank outage.

31
00:02:57.825 --> 00:03:05.697
Gateway-level failover fixed the gateway incident (54%, then 91%) but did nothing for the bank outage.

32
00:03:07.047 --> 00:03:17.628
Health per (gateway, bank), plus steering customers of the failed bank to another method, raised success during the bank outage from 79% to 89%.

33
00:03:17.908 --> 00:03:23.049
Auto-retrying on the second gateway brought the gateway incident to 97%.

34
00:03:23.329 --> 00:03:24.311
Reading time:

35
00:03:24.591 --> 00:03:26.511
120 min + code.

36
00:03:27.861 --> 00:03:28.714
Part 1.

37
00:03:28.994 --> 00:03:30.914
Introduction and Why It Matters.

38
00:03:32.164 --> 00:03:34.553
A story: the month the money didn't add up.

39
00:03:34.833 --> 00:03:44.753
The grocery platform's first payment integration was a tutorial: create an order at the gateway, open its checkout, and mark the order paid when the app says success.

40
00:03:45.033 --> 00:03:47.615
Within a quarter the finance team escalated:

41
00:03:47.895 --> 00:03:49.004
Double shipments.

42
00:03:49.284 --> 00:03:55.129
The app's success callback and the gateway's webhook both marked orders paid; each triggered fulfilment.

43
00:03:55.409 --> 00:03:57.777
About one order in twenty-five shipped twice.

44
00:03:58.057 --> 00:03:59.359
Charged, no order.

45
00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:01.324
Some webhooks never arrived.

46
00:04:01.604 --> 00:04:05.572
Customers were debited, saw payment pending forever, and called support.

47
00:04:05.852 --> 00:04:07.516
Some paid twice trying again.

48
00:04:08.866 --> 00:04:10.039
Free groceries.

49
00:04:10.319 --> 00:04:13.007
A modified app sent success for failed payments.

50
00:04:13.287 --> 00:04:16.701
A few dozen orders shipped unpaid before anyone noticed.

51
00:04:16.981 --> 00:04:17.962
Wallet drift.

52
00:04:18.242 --> 00:04:24.045
Cashback and refunds went to a wallet whose balance was a column updated in application code.

53
00:04:24.325 --> 00:04:31.173
Under load the balances drifted; the total of all wallets no longer matched the money the company owed its customers.

54
00:04:31.453 --> 00:04:32.541
The settlement gap.

55
00:04:32.821 --> 00:04:37.514
The gateway deposited 3.9 lakh rupees less than expected one day.

56
00:04:37.794 --> 00:04:44.898
Nobody could say why: late settlements, fees, a duplicate, manual refunds from the dashboard, or a real loss.

57
00:04:46.248 --> 00:04:48.744
A bank outage looked like a gateway outage.

58
00:04:49.024 --> 00:04:54.379
When one large bank's UPI went down, the team switched all traffic to the second gateway.

59
00:04:54.659 --> 00:04:58.499
It changed nothing, and nearly broke the second gateway's rate limits.

60
00:04:58.779 --> 00:05:04.368
Payments are where distributed-systems theory meets money, regulation and customers' trust.

61
00:05:04.648 --> 00:05:20.093
Every message can be duplicated, delayed, lost or forged; the only defence is a design in which every state change is idempotent, every movement of money is recorded as a balanced entry, and every day ends with proof that the books match the bank.

62
00:05:21.443 --> 00:05:22.126
Quote:

63
00:05:22.406 --> 00:05:24.070
The thesis of this chapter.

64
00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:33.118
A payment system is a state machine per payment, a double-entry ledger for money, and reconciliation as the source of truth about the outside world.

65
00:05:33.398 --> 00:05:39.094
Money moves only as immutable, balanced journal entries under concurrency control (L1).

66
00:05:39.374 --> 00:05:51.214
Payment state moves only through guarded, idempotent transitions driven by verified gateway events, never by the client, and pending states are resolved by asking the gateway (L2).

67
00:05:51.494 --> 00:06:00.497
Reconciliation against settlement files is not an accounting afterthought; it is how the system discovers its own bugs and the gateway's (L3).

68
00:06:00.777 --> 00:06:05.705
And routing must know whose failure it is, gateway or bank, before it acts (L4).

69
00:06:07.055 --> 00:06:09.359
Where this lives in the grocery platform.

