683
00:48:35.927 --> 00:48:37.271
Interview Q and A.

684
00:48:38.521 --> 00:48:39.353
Beginner.

685
00:48:39.633 --> 00:48:40.657
Question 1.

686
00:48:40.937 --> 00:48:44.692
Design a payment system for an e-commerce app, the main components?

687
00:48:44.972 --> 00:48:46.337
Thirty-second answer:

688
00:48:46.617 --> 00:48:51.972
A payment service with a state machine per payment (created, then captured, then refunded),

689
00:48:52.252 --> 00:49:13.095
integrating one or more gateways via hosted checkout; a webhook receiver that verifies and dedupes events; a status poller for unknown outcomes; a double-entry ledger; an outbox to notify checkout and fulfilment; daily reconciliation against settlement files; and security controls (tokenisation, signatures, audit).

690
00:49:14.445 --> 00:49:15.447
Question 2.

691
00:49:15.727 --> 00:49:17.647
How do you prevent double charges?

692
00:49:17.927 --> 00:49:19.293
Thirty-second answer:

693
00:49:19.573 --> 00:49:42.229
Idempotency at every hop: an idempotency key per checkout attempt on our API; our payment id as the gateway order's receipt so the gateway dedupes order creation; one gateway order per checkout, reused on retries; payment attempts only through the gateway's checkout for that order; and refunds for any duplicate captures found by reconciliation.

694
00:49:43.579 --> 00:49:44.624
Question 3.

695
00:49:44.904 --> 00:49:48.168
What is double-entry bookkeeping, and why use it in software?

696
00:49:48.448 --> 00:49:49.813
Thirty-second answer:

697
00:49:50.093 --> 00:49:57.304
Every movement of money is recorded as an entry with legs that sum to zero, a debit somewhere and a credit somewhere else.

698
00:49:57.584 --> 00:50:00.741
The ledger is append-only; balances are derived.

699
00:50:01.021 --> 00:50:08.595
It makes errors detectable (entries that don't balance), gives a full audit trail, and lets you reconstruct any balance at any time.

700
00:50:09.945 --> 00:50:11.011
Question 4.

701
00:50:11.291 --> 00:50:13.937
Why not trust the payment success callback from the app?

702
00:50:14.217 --> 00:50:15.582
Thirty-second answer:

703
00:50:15.862 --> 00:50:23.606
The app runs on a device you don't control; responses can be forged or replayed, and network issues mean it may never arrive.

704
00:50:23.886 --> 00:50:31.075
Use it only to trigger a server-side status check; the gateway's signed webhook or status API is the truth.

705
00:50:32.425 --> 00:50:33.428
Intermediate.

706
00:50:33.708 --> 00:50:34.817
Question 5.

707
00:50:35.097 --> 00:50:37.039
How do you handle webhooks correctly?

708
00:50:37.319 --> 00:50:38.684
Thirty-second answer:

709
00:50:38.964 --> 00:50:59.849
Verify the signature, store the event id with a unique constraint and process in the same transaction as the state change, apply only allowed transitions (guarded UPDATE), treat events as at-least-once and unordered, respond 200 quickly (queue heavy work), and never rely on webhooks alone, poll non-terminal payments.

710
00:51:01.199 --> 00:51:02.309
Question 6.

711
00:51:02.589 --> 00:51:05.319
A payment's outcome is unknown after a timeout.

712
00:51:05.599 --> 00:51:06.602
What do you do?

713
00:51:06.882 --> 00:51:08.247
Thirty-second answer:

714
00:51:08.527 --> 00:51:10.618
Don't guess and don't blindly retry.

715
00:51:10.898 --> 00:51:19.154
Record it as pending, query the gateway's status by our reference (with backoff), and apply the result through the same guarded transition.

716
00:51:19.434 --> 00:51:27.754
If still unknown after the window, mark it NEEDSATTENTION and let reconciliation resolve it; show the customer payment processing.

717
00:51:29.104 --> 00:51:30.192
Question 7.

718
00:51:30.472 --> 00:51:31.880
How do refunds work?

719
00:51:32.160 --> 00:51:33.525
Thirty-second answer:

720
00:51:33.805 --> 00:51:53.773
A refund is its own idempotent object with a state machine, created against a captured payment, checked under a lock so total refunds never exceed the capture, sent to the gateway with our refund id, confirmed by webhook or polling, recorded as ledger entries, and reconciled in the settlement file (negative rows).

721
00:51:55.123 --> 00:51:56.147
Question 8.

722
00:51:56.427 --> 00:51:59.393
How do you represent money in code and databases?

723
00:51:59.673 --> 00:52:01.038
Thirty-second answer:

724
00:52:01.318 --> 00:52:10.001
Integer minor units (paise) with an explicit currency, or a decimal type with fixed scale in the database; never binary floating point.

725
00:52:10.281 --> 00:52:18.046
Define rounding rules (banker's rounding or as contracts specify) for fees and taxes, and apply them once, in one place.

726
00:52:19.396 --> 00:52:20.463
Question 9.

727
00:52:20.743 --> 00:52:23.345
Why and how do you reconcile with the gateway?

728
00:52:23.625 --> 00:52:24.991
Thirty-second answer:

729
00:52:25.271 --> 00:52:30.796
Because messages get lost, duplicated or wrong, and the gateway and banks make mistakes too.

730
00:52:31.076 --> 00:52:44.132
Match each settlement row to the ledger by reference, with rules for timing, rounding and expected fees, and produce a categorised exception queue with rupee impact; do three-way matching with bank statements.

731
00:52:45.482 --> 00:52:46.250
Senior.

732
00:52:46.530 --> 00:52:47.575
Question 10.

733
00:52:47.855 --> 00:52:50.565
How do you keep a ledger correct under concurrency?

734
00:52:50.845 --> 00:52:52.210
Thirty-second answer:

735
00:52:52.490 --> 00:52:59.871
Append-only balanced entries; a balance row per account updated in the same transaction under a row lock (SELECT...

736
00:53:00.151 --> 00:53:13.271
FOR UPDATE) with checks (non-negative); serializable isolation only where contention is low; hot internal accounts split into sub-accounts or aggregated asynchronously; nightly invariant checks.

737
00:53:14.621 --> 00:53:15.752
Question 11.

738
00:53:16.032 --> 00:53:18.891
How do you route payments across multiple gateways?

739
00:53:19.171 --> 00:53:20.536
Thirty-second answer:

740
00:53:20.816 --> 00:53:42.192
Track success rates and error classes per (gateway, method, issuer bank) over a short window; route each payment to the cheapest healthy option; keep a probe share on degraded paths; when every gateway fails for a bank, steer the customer to another method; auto-retry on a second gateway only for pre-debit gateway errors, with idempotent orders.

741
00:53:43.542 --> 00:53:44.630
Question 12.

742
00:53:44.910 --> 00:53:47.555
How does fulfilment learn about payments reliably?

743
00:53:47.835 --> 00:53:49.201
Thirty-second answer:

744
00:53:49.481 --> 00:54:00.979
The payment service writes an outbox event in the same transaction as the transition to CAPTURED; a relay publishes it; checkout and fulfilment consume idempotently by payment id.

745
00:54:01.259 --> 00:54:04.758
Fulfilment never polls or listens to webhooks directly.

746
00:54:06.108 --> 00:54:07.388
Question 13.

747
00:54:07.668 --> 00:54:11.935
What are the PCI DSS implications, and how do you minimise them?

748
00:54:12.215 --> 00:54:13.580
Thirty-second answer:

749
00:54:13.860 --> 00:54:21.135
Handling card numbers puts your systems in PCI DSS scope (the strictest levels if you store or process them).

750
00:54:21.415 --> 00:54:35.580
Minimise scope by using the gateway's hosted checkout or SDK so cards go directly to the gateway, store only tokens (network or gateway tokens, as RBI requires), and never log payment payloads.

751
00:54:36.930 --> 00:54:38.125
Question 14.

752
00:54:38.405 --> 00:54:41.583
How do you handle a festival sale with 10x payments?

753
00:54:41.863 --> 00:54:43.229
Thirty-second answer:

754
00:54:43.509 --> 00:55:05.162
Pre-scale the payment service and webhook receivers, confirm gateway capacity and rate limits, spread traffic across gateways by health, queue webhook processing with backpressure, keep the ledger and state machine transactions small, watch success rates per bank and method in real time, and have customer-facing fallbacks (other methods, COD) and runbooks.

755
00:55:06.512 --> 00:55:07.408
Architect.

756
00:55:07.688 --> 00:55:08.883
Question 15.

757
00:55:09.163 --> 00:55:13.643
Design the payments platform for a national grocery marketplace end to end.

758
00:55:13.923 --> 00:55:15.288
Thirty-second answer:

759
00:55:15.568 --> 00:55:26.725
Payment service with guarded state machines for payments and refunds; multi-gateway router with health per (gateway, method, bank); hosted checkout and tokenisation; verified,

760
00:55:27.005 --> 00:55:45.245
deduped webhooks plus status polling; double-entry ledger with locked projections; outbox to checkout and fulfilment; wallet as ledger accounts; daily three-way reconciliation with an exception queue; vendor payouts; audit, approvals and fraud controls; observability by segment.

761
00:55:46.595 --> 00:55:47.790
Question 16.

762
00:55:48.070 --> 00:55:50.886
How would you scale the ledger beyond one database?

763
00:55:51.166 --> 00:55:52.531
Thirty-second answer:

764
00:55:52.811 --> 00:56:13.803
Shard by account (customer wallets), keep each entry's legs within a shard where possible, and use a saga with a clearing account for cross-shard transfers; aggregate hot internal accounts from per-shard sub-accounts; keep a global, append-only event stream for reporting and reconciliation; verify invariants per shard and globally.

765
00:56:15.153 --> 00:56:16.497
Question 17.

766
00:56:16.777 --> 00:56:18.569
Build versus buy for payments?

767
00:56:18.849 --> 00:56:20.215
Thirty-second answer:

768
00:56:20.495 --> 00:56:27.193
Buy gateways (and possibly a payment orchestration layer) for method coverage, compliance and bank connectivity.

769
00:56:27.473 --> 00:56:39.996
Build the parts that encode your business and your correctness: the state machine around your orders, the ledger, reconciliation, routing policy and customer steering, they differ by business and are where losses hide.

770
00:56:41.346 --> 00:56:42.519
Question 18.

771
00:56:42.799 --> 00:56:48.538
How do you design for regulatory change (tokenisation, data localisation, e-mandates)?

772
00:56:48.818 --> 00:56:50.183
Thirty-second answer:

773
00:56:50.463 --> 00:57:07.551
Isolate gateway- and regulation-specific logic in adapters, keep sensitive data out of your systems (tokens only), store payment data in-region, make consent and mandate flows explicit states, and track regulatory deadlines as architecture work with owners.

774
00:57:08.901 --> 00:57:10.139
Question 19.

775
00:57:10.419 --> 00:57:12.147
How do you test a payment system?

776
00:57:12.427 --> 00:57:13.792
Thirty-second answer:

777
00:57:14.072 --> 00:57:36.493
A gateway simulator (like L2's) with fault injection, duplicates, reordering, drops, timeouts, tampered callbacks, plus property tests on the ledger (every entry balances, balances equal sums), concurrency tests (L1), reconciliation tests with synthetic discrepancies (L3), gateway sandbox tests, and production canaries with small real payments.

778
00:57:37.843 --> 00:57:38.953
Question 20.

779
00:57:39.233 --> 00:57:41.750
What metrics tell you the payment system is healthy?

780
00:57:42.030 --> 00:57:43.395
Thirty-second answer:

781
00:57:43.675 --> 00:58:07.142
Success rate by method, gateway and bank (excluding customer-side failures); time to confirmed status; captures not fulfilled or refunded after N minutes (should be ~0); duplicate captures; reconciliation exceptions by category and value; ledger invariant violations (must be 0); refund turnaround; fees versus expected; chargeback and fraud rates.

782
00:58:08.492 --> 00:58:09.388
Part 9.

783
00:58:09.668 --> 00:58:11.076
Summary and Practice.

784
00:58:12.326 --> 00:58:13.414
Key takeaways.

785
00:58:13.694 --> 00:58:19.262
Double-entry, append-only ledger with locked balance projections; integer paise (L1).

786
00:58:19.542 --> 00:58:24.129
Guarded state machine; the client's success is a hint, never the truth (L2).

787
00:58:24.409 --> 00:58:29.209
Webhooks are at-least-once, unordered, lossy: dedupe, guard, and poll (L2).

788
00:58:29.489 --> 00:58:36.806
Reconciliation finds the bugs: line matching with rules, three-way, exception queue with rupee impact (L3).

789
00:58:37.086 --> 00:58:43.081
Route by (gateway, bank), steer customers on bank outages, retry only pre-debit (L4).

790
00:58:44.431 --> 00:58:47.695
Idempotency at every hop, tokenisation, audit.

